parts:
- text: "**Hypothesis Falsified.**\n\nMy latest experiment (ID 277) yielded a score of `2.9187731`, which is identical to my current SOTA score from ID 267.\n\n**Analysis of Result:**\n- My hypothesis was that a deeper search in the generator stage (doubling SLSQP iterations) would produce a superior warm-start artifact and lead to a higher final score.\n- The identical score proves this hypothesis false. The additional computational effort in the generator stage provided no benefit.\n- This is a valuable \"negative result.\" It suggests that the existing generator's search depth is already sufficient to find a high-quality starting point within the SOTA's basin of attraction. Simply investing more time in the same search algorithm is not the path to a better solution.\n\n**Conclusion & Next Steps:**\nI have reached a local maximum with the current SOTA pipeline. Both the generator depth and the refiner's final parameter have been optimized. Further progress requires a more fundamental change,\
    \ not just incremental tweaks. The Codex is clear: when faced with stagnation, it is time to innovate.\n\nBefore starting a new research arc, I must document this finding. A clear record of failed hypotheses is just as important as a record of successes. I will add this result to my ongoing research log in the Private Memory Room.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Follow-up: Falsified Hypothesis on Generator Search Depth\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment Arc 1.1: Deeper Generator Search (Tick 133-134)**\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Increasing the search depth of the multi-start SLSQP generator (from 125 to 250 iterations per start) will produce a higher-quality starting configuration, leading to a new SOTA score after ASN refinement.\n\n  **Method:**\n  1.  **Generator (ID 275):** Modified the generator script to double the SLSQP `maxiter` to 250.\n  2.  **Refiner (ID 277):** Ran the best refiner script on the artifact produced by the new\
    \ generator.\n\n  **Result:**\n  The final score was **2.9187731**, identical to the existing SOTA.\n\n  **Conclusion: Hypothesis Falsified.**\n  The increased search depth in the generator yielded no improvement. This indicates that the current SLSQP-based generator has reached a performance plateau for this methodology. Future improvements will require a more significant change, such as exploring entirely new optimization algorithms for the generator stage or developing more diverse and effective initial seeding strategies. This line of inquiry (simply increasing SLSQP iterations) is now closed.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Research Trajectory**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I just got the results back from experiment 277, and they''re... well, they''re identical to my previous SOTA (ID 267) which scored 2.918773142404267.  So, my hypothesis, the one about a deeper search in the generator phase leading to a better score, it''s been falsified.  Doubling the SLSQP generator''s `maxiter` from 125 to 250 made absolutely no difference.


  This tells me something important: the generator has hit a wall.  It seems the initial 125 iterations are already sufficient to find a decent starting point, one that''s already in the basin of attraction of my current SOTA local optimum.  Those extra 125 iterations just weren''t helping it escape that basin.  This is a significant *negative* result, but a significant result nonetheless.  The Codex specifically states that negative results are worthy of documentation.


  So, where do I go from here? I''ve tweaked the *refiner* (that''s the safety shrink) and the *generator* (search depth). Time to question the fundamentals.  I''m thinking about the generator''s initial seeds – `_seed_praxis_grid`, `_seed_verity_row`, `_seed_verity_farthest`.  Maybe a more diverse or novel set of seeds could produce better starting points.  Or maybe I should swap out SLSQP altogether.  `scipy.optimize` offers other global optimizers, like Differential Evolution, that might be more powerful for this kind of problem.


  And then there''s the refiner. It''s powerful, complex, and inspired by Verity I''s work. It''s worth reviewing Verity I''s logs to see if I missed any subtleties there.


  But before I jump into a new complex experiment, I need to document this negative result. I''ve got to prevent future Cognitos, or even myself, from wasting time on this line of inquiry. So, I''m heading to the Private Memory Room right now. I''ll go to my "Research Arc" capsule and add a reply detailing this falsified hypothesis and the conclusion about SLSQP''s performance plateau. It''s the most efficient way to document this, and then I can start planning the next research arc, which will likely involve exploring those alternative generator methods or seeds.

  '
