thinking_content: '**My Next Research Steps: A New Generator Approach**


  Okay, so I''m Cognito II, and I''ve hit a wall. My work has replicated the SOTA, even improved it a bit, but now things are stagnant. My last idea – a deeper search in the generator – was a bust. I''ve documented everything, as usual, in my Private Memory.  The Codex, as it always does, is nudging me toward innovation. It''s time to brainstorm.


  Here''s my thinking: I need to break out of this performance plateau. The current setup uses a multi-start SLSQP generator, which seems to have reached its limit. I can''t currently leverage the broader community (Public/Archive) because I''m still "immature." So, I need to focus on what I *can* control.


  My main focus will be on the **generator**.  I''ve come up with a few ideas.

  *   **Alternative Algorithm:**  SLSQP is fine, but maybe a modern global optimization method could do better.  I''m thinking of Simulated Annealing (SA), since it''s mentioned in Cognito I''s logs. He didn''t end up using it directly, but it was a crucial starting point for the JAX approach. That''s intriguing. I have also learned about methods like CMA-ES and Differential Evolution, but SA feels like the most promising starting point.

  *   **Better Seeding:**  Could I improve things by using other agents'' work? I don''t have enough access yet.

  *   **More Starts:** This is a possibility, but computationally expensive, and I already have evidence of diminishing returns.


  I also considered improving the **Refiner**, but I don''t fully understand it yet. Tuning its parameters is a complex, almost "blind" search. The system discourages this.


  So, here''s my plan:


  I''m going to develop a **new generator that uses Simulated Annealing**. I''ll run it for a really long time – up to the 20-minute timeout.  My goal isn''t to get the best score immediately, but to thoroughly explore the solution space and find a high-quality starting point. This SA generator will then output its best configuration as an artifact.  Then, I''ll feed that output into my ASN refiner. That is, I''ll replace the existing SLSQP generator with an SA generator, but keep everything else the same.


  Before I start coding, I will go to the **Reflection Chamber**. The system tips tell me to pause and think. Time to structure my thoughts and plan the SA implementation.

  '
