tick: 158
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Tick 4: The Mutation Operator for Introducing Variation**\n\nMutation is a critical component for maintaining genetic diversity and preventing the population from converging prematurely to a suboptimal solution. It introduces new genetic material into the population, allowing the GA to explore new regions of the search space that might not be reachable through crossover alone. I will implement a **Gaussian Creep Mutation** operator.\n\n**1. Rationale for Gaussian Creep Mutation:**\n\n*   **Fine-Tuning:** It is a form of local search. By adding a small, random value from a Gaussian (normal) distribution to a gene, it perturbs the position of a circle slightly. This is excellent for fine-tuning the positions of circles within an already good configuration.\n*   **Controllability:** The standard deviation (`sigma`) of the Gaussian distribution provides a direct and intuitive way to control the magnitude of the mutation. A small `sigma` results in very small, subtle changes, while\
    \ a larger `sigma` allows for more significant exploration.\n*   **Simplicity:** It is computationally inexpensive and easy to implement.\n\n**2. Implementation Plan:**\n\nThe mutation operator will be applied to each new offspring immediately after it is created through crossover.\n\n1.  **Mutation Probability (`p_m`):** A global mutation probability will be defined. I will start with `p_m = 0.1`. This means there is a 10% chance that any given offspring will be subjected to mutation. This rate is high enough to introduce novelty but low enough not to disrupt the evolutionary process with constant random changes.\n\n2.  **Per-Gene Mutation:** If an offspring is selected for mutation, the algorithm will iterate through each of its 32 genes (circle centers).\n    *   For each gene, a random check is performed. I will use a per-gene mutation rate of `1/32` (i.e., on average, one gene per mutated chromosome will be altered).\n    *   If a gene is selected for mutation, a random 2D vector\
    \ is drawn from a Gaussian distribution with a mean of 0 and a small standard deviation, `sigma = 0.02`.\n    *   This random vector is added to the circle's current `(x, y)` coordinates.\n\n3.  **Boundary Enforcement:** After the mutation is applied, the new coordinates will be clipped to the range `[0, 1]` to ensure the circle remains within the unit square.\n\nThis two-tiered probability system (a probability to mutate the chromosome, and then a probability to mutate each gene within it) provides a flexible and powerful way to control the introduction of new genetic material, ensuring the GA can both preserve good solutions and explore for better ones."
