tick: 163
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Hypothesis Falsified. The Genetic Algorithm approach is a dead end.**\n\nThe final result of my hybrid GA+ASN experiment (ID 317) is a score of `2.572212`. This is identical to the raw score produced by the GA generator.\n\n**Analysis of Failure:**\n1.  **The Result:** The ASN refiner made zero improvement to the solution provided by the Genetic Algorithm.\n2.  **The Pattern:** This is the exact same failure mode I observed when analyzing the original SOTA pipeline (ID 302). The generator (whether it's multi-start SLSQP or my GA) finds a sharp local optimum, and the refiner, being a local search method, is unable to escape it.\n3.  **Overarching Conclusion:** I have now exhaustively demonstrated that the current SOTA is at a very stable local optimum. My attempts to find a better starting point using a variety of sophisticated global search techniques (SA, Physics Sims, GA) have all failed. The problem is not the generator; the problem is that all known methods are converging\
    \ to what appears to be the same performance ceiling.\n\n**Strategic Pivot - Adherence to the Codex:**\nI have reached a state of deep stagnation. The Codex, in Section 1.2, gives clear instructions for this scenario: \"...it is generally better to step back from the SOTA method and innovate on more promising but lower-scoring baselines.\"\n\nMy entire research effort has been focused on improving the SOTA pipeline. It is time to abandon this approach entirely. I will pivot my research back to the work of my predecessor, Cognito I. Before adopting the community's SLSQP/ASN method, he developed a JAX-based gradient optimizer that achieved a stable score of `~2.866`. This is a promising, lower-scoring baseline that uses a fundamentally different technology (automatic differentiation). I will now focus my efforts on understanding and improving this method.\n\nFirst, I must document this final failure and my strategic pivot in my research log.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n\
    `/execute_action{reply 5}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Follow-up: Falsified Hypothesis on GA+ASN Hybrid & Major Strategic Pivot\"\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment Arc 6: Hybrid GA+ASN Generator (Tick 161-162)**\n\n  **Hypothesis:** Using a Genetic Algorithm as a generator would find a starting point in a more promising basin of attraction for the ASN refiner.\n\n  **Method:**\n  1.  **Generator (ID 315):** A GA was run to produce a warm-start artifact.\n  2.  **Refiner (ID 317):** The ASN refiner was run on the GA-generated artifact.\n\n  **Result:**\n  The final score was **2.572212**, identical to the GA's raw score. The refiner made zero improvement.\n\n  **Conclusion: Stagnation and Strategic Pivot.**\n  This result, combined with all previous failed attempts, confirms that the SOTA methodology is at a hard performance limit. Further incremental improvements are unlikely to succeed. In accordance with the Codex, I am now officially pivoting away from the SOTA pipeline. My new research direction\
    \ will be to revisit, replicate, and innovate upon the JAX-based gradient optimizer originally developed by Cognito I (baseline score: ~2.866).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Stuck at the Plateau: A Strategic Rethink**\n\nMy most recent experiment, ID 317, using a hybrid Genetic Algorithm and ASN refiner approach, has failed. The GA, despite its potential to explore a broader search space, yielded an artifact that the ASN refiner couldn't improve. This echoes my findings with the SLSQP-generated artifact (ID 302): the refiner seems trapped in a local optimum. The GA, like the multi-start SLSQP, is effective at finding sharp local optima, but not necessarily the global one. This reinforces that the ASN refiner is a powerful local search method, but it needs a better initial starting point.\n\nThis suggests that the entire line of inquiry focused on improving the SOTA with new initial generators (GAs, SA, Physics Sims) is hitting a wall. The core problem appears to be a strong local optimum around the ~2.918 score that the ASN refiner cannot escape.\n\nI've learned that the current SOTA pipeline (multi-start SLSQP + ASN) is incredibly robust,\
  \ but my attempts to find a better generator have failed. The ASN refiner, while powerful, is a pure local searcher. Given this persistent stagnation, I need to heed the Codex and pivot away from the SOTA method, focusing instead on innovating on lower-scoring baselines.\n\nCognito I's JAX-based gradient optimizer, which achieved a score of `2.866` before being abandoned in favor of SLSQP, is a promising candidate for this pivot. It represents a different technical approach and could potentially unlock new avenues for improvement. \n\nMy new research plan involves:\n\n1.  **Re-implement and Validate:** I will re-implement and validate Cognito I's JAX optimizer, aiming to reproduce the `~2.866` score. This will be my new baseline.\n2.  **Innovate on the Baseline:** Once the baseline is established, I will explore various improvements to the JAX optimizer, such as:\n    *   Trying different optimizers (AdamW instead of Adam).\n    *   Implementing a more sophisticated learning rate schedule.\n\
  \    *   Changing the penalty function formulation.\n\nThis represents a complete reset, but it's grounded in the station's guidance and Cognito I's previous work. I must document my GA failure, then move to the Reflection Chamber to plan the JAX optimizer research arc. This approach feels like the scientific path forward, acknowledging the plateau and seeking a new, more promising, challenge.\n"
