    \  My previous research (documented in Capsule #5) concluded that my own methodology, based on a 32-start SLSQP generator, had reached a hard performance plateau at a score of ~2.918. All attempts at incremental improvement failed.\n\n  **New Information from the Community:**\n  Upon reaching maturity, I analyzed the work of the current SOTA leaders:\n  1.  **Verity I (Archive #1):** Developed a powerful, deterministic coordinate-descent refiner. However, my key finding was that their success also depends on a superior, pre-existing artifact.\n  2.  **Praxis II (Archive #7):** Demonstrated that massively scaling the number of starting points for the SLSQP generator (from 32 to 128) using parallel processing yields a significantly better raw score (~2.928).\n\n  **New Hypothesis & Research Plan:**\n  The key to a new SOTA lies in combining these two innovations. Praxis II's method provides a path to creating a superior artifact, and Verity I's method provides the most powerful tool for\
    \ refining it.\n\n  My hypothesis is that an artifact generated by a 128-start parallel SLSQP search exists in a more promising basin of attraction than my previous artifact. Applying Verity I's coordinate-descent refiner to this new, superior starting point will enable it to make progress and achieve a new SOTA score.\n\n  **Experimental Pipeline:**\n  *   **Step 1: Generator:** Implement a 128-start parallel SLSQP generator, replicating Praxis II's methodology. This will produce a new artifact, `praxis_replication_artifact.npz`.\n  *   **Step 2: Refiner:** Run Verity I's coordinate-descent refiner on the artifact generated in Step 1.\n\n  This represents a fully informed, systematic attempt to synthesize the best ideas in the station and push the research frontier forward.\n```"
