tick: 189
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in my Private Memory Room. I will now create a new capsule to draft the paper summarizing my final and most powerful method for the circle packing problem. This paper will serve as a formal contribution to the station's collective knowledge, as mandated by the Codex.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: A Massively Parallel Two-Stage SLSQP Method for Circle Packing\"\ntags: [\"paper draft\", \"slsqp\", \"parallel computing\", \"circle packing\"]\nabstract: \"This paper details a high-performance method for the 32-circle packing problem, achieving a verifiable score of 2.927199. The method is a two-stage algorithm featuring a massively parallel generator and a high-precision refiner, both using the SLSQP optimizer. The first stage leverages parallel processing to conduct a broad search over 128 unique starting configurations. The second stage performs a deep, local refinement on the single best candidate found. We present this method as a robust, scalable,\
    \ and near-SOTA baseline, and discuss the evidence for a performance plateau inherent to this class of optimizer.\"\ncontent: |\n  **Paper: A Massively Parallel Two-Stage SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper presents a robust and highly effective methodology for the 32-circle packing problem. Building upon the foundational work on multi-start SLSQP methods within the station (Praxis I, Archive #5; Praxis II, Archive #7), our work represents the culmination of this research direction, achieving a verifiable score of 2.927199 (Evaluation ID 364). Our contribution is a refined two-stage pipeline that combines a massively parallel exploratory search with a deep, high-precision local refinement, demonstrating the power of computational scale in navigating this complex optimization landscape.\n\n  **2. Methodology**\n  Our algorithm is a two-stage process:\n\n  *   **Stage 1: Massively Parallel Multi-Start Generation:** This stage focuses\
    \ on broad exploration. We run 128 independent instances of the SLSQP optimizer in parallel using Python's `multiprocessing` library. Each instance is seeded with a unique initial configuration generated from a mix of three strategies (perturbed grid, farthest-point, and triangular lattice) to ensure diversity. Each parallel run is executed for a moderate number of iterations (350) to quickly identify promising regions of the solution space.\n\n  *   **Stage 2: High-Precision Refinement:** The single best solution vector from all 128 parallel runs in Stage 1 is selected. This candidate is then subjected to a second, much deeper SLSQP optimization run, configured for high precision with 2000 iterations. This stage serves to polish the best-found solution to its local optimum.\n\n  A final safety shrink factor of `(1.0 - 1e-8)` is applied to the final radii to ensure robust validation against floating-point errors.\n\n  **3. Verifiable Results**\n  The definitive implementation of this\
    \ method is found in **Evaluation ID 364**, which achieved a score of **2.927198894812512**. Our research also demonstrated that the high-precision refinement stage (Stage 2) provided no improvement over the best result from Stage 1, as shown by the STDOUT log of the evaluation. This provides strong evidence that the SLSQP optimizer, for this problem, finds extremely sharp local optima from which it cannot escape, even with a much longer runtime.\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  We present this two-stage, massively parallel SLSQP method as a powerful and highly competitive baseline, representing a performance ceiling for this specific class of algorithm. While not the absolute station SOTA, its score is robustly near the top of the leaderboard and was achieved through a systematic synthesis of community-developed best practices. We conclude that further significant improvements are unlikely to come from refining this methodology further. Instead, future research should focus on fundamentally\
    \ different generator approaches that can discover the distinct and slightly superior basin of attraction occupied by the current SOTA artifact, or on entirely new optimization paradigms.\n```"
