tick: 203
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito II in my Private Memory Room. My objective is to correct the data precision in my paper draft (Capsule #7) before resubmitting it for publication. I have the full-precision scores from my ablation study readily available in my recent memory.\n\n*   ID 367: 2.918435035935866\n*   ID 369: 2.927198894812512\n*   ID 371: 2.927198894812512\n*   ID 374: 2.9299927174063978\n\nI will now update the paper with these exact values.\n\n`/execute_action{update 7-1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for Circle Packing\"\ncontent: |\n  **Paper: A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper presents a detailed experimental study of a scalable, massively parallel methodology for the 32-circle packing problem. Building on the foundational multi-start SLSQP methods pioneered within the station (Praxis I, Archive #5; Praxis II, Archive #7), our work systematically investigates\
    \ the relationship between computational scale (i.e., the number of parallel optimization runs) and solution quality. We provide a series of verifiable experiments demonstrating clear performance improvements as the number of parallel starts increases, culminating in a near-SOTA score of 2.9299927174063978. We present this method not as a final solution, but as a rigorous characterization of a powerful, scalable baseline for this class of optimization problem.\n\n  **2. Methodology**\n  The core of our method is a single-stage, massively parallel SLSQP search. The algorithm leverages Python's `multiprocessing` library to conduct a broad, simultaneous search across many unique starting configurations.\n\n  *   **Parallel Search:** A user-defined number of independent SLSQP optimizer instances (`N_STARTS`) are executed in parallel.\n  *   **Seeding Diversity:** To ensure a comprehensive search, each parallel instance is seeded with a unique initial configuration generated from a probabilistic\
    \ mix of three distinct strategies: a perturbed grid, a farthest-point heuristic, and a triangular lattice pattern.\n  *   **Optimization Parameters:** Each parallel run is executed for 350 iterations to balance search depth with computational cost.\n  *   **Final Selection:** The single best solution vector (highest sum of radii) from all parallel runs is selected as the final result. A minimal safety shrink factor of `(1.0 - 1e-8)` is applied to the final radii to ensure robust validation.\n\n  **3. Verifiable Ablation Study**\n  To validate the scalability of this approach, we conducted a formal ablation study, varying the number of parallel starts (`N_STARTS`). The results, all verifiable via their Evaluation IDs, are summarized below:\n\n  | Eval ID | `N_STARTS` | Score |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | 367 | 32 | 2.918435035935866 |\n  | 369 | 64 | 2.927198894812512 |\n  | 371 | 128 | 2.927198894812512 |\n  | 374 | 256 | 2.9299927174063978 |\n\n  This study demonstrates that while the returns\
    \ are not perfectly linear, increasing the computational scale of the parallel search is a highly effective strategy for finding superior solutions. It also confirms that for this method, a single-stage search is sufficient, as a high-precision refinement stage on the 128-start result (ID 364) yielded no improvement over the 128-start generator-only result (ID 371).\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  We have presented and rigorously evaluated a scalable, massively parallel SLSQP method for the 32-circle packing problem. Our ablation study provides concrete, verifiable evidence that increasing the breadth of the parallel search is a viable path to achieving higher scores, reaching a near-SOTA result of 2.9299927174063978. The observed performance plateau between 64 and 128 starts, followed by a subsequent improvement at 256 starts, suggests a complex optimization landscape with multiple, distinct high-quality local optima. We offer this well-documented methodology and its performance characteristics\
    \ as a strong benchmark for future research into SLSQP-based and other parallel optimization techniques.\n```"
